WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.543
Quantum risk assessment should be specific, not one-size-fits-all.

2
00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:17.709
Useful factors include data sensitivity, data lifetime, system lifetime, cryptographic dependency, exposure, adversary interest, replacement difficulty, vendor readiness, and business impact.

3
00:00:17.709 --> 00:00:29.412
A public website with short-lived data may not have the same urgency as a sensitive archive, a firmware signing system, or an industrial device with a twenty-year deployment cycle.

4
00:00:29.412 --> 00:00:35.264
Data sensitivity asks what harm would occur if the information were exposed in the future.

5
00:00:35.264 --> 00:00:39.421
Data lifetime asks how long confidentiality must be preserved.

6
00:00:39.421 --> 00:00:43.271
System lifetime asks how long the system will remain in use.

7
00:00:43.271 --> 00:00:50.201
Cryptographic dependency asks whether the system relies on quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms.

8
00:00:50.201 --> 00:00:56.899
Exposure asks whether traffic or artifacts can be collected or tampered with by relevant adversaries.

9
00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:00.441
Replacement difficulty is often the deciding factor.

10
00:01:00.441 --> 00:01:15.070
If a system can be updated quickly through a well-tested library and normal deployment pipeline, it may be lower priority than a system with hard-coded cryptography, vendor-controlled firmware, or regulatory constraints.

11
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:21.846
Vendor readiness matters because many organizations cannot change product cryptography on their own.

12
00:01:21.846 --> 00:01:26.927
Business impact matters because migration failures can disrupt operations.

13
00:01:26.927 --> 00:01:30.238
A good assessment produces a prioritized roadmap.

14
00:01:30.238 --> 00:01:39.554
It should identify high-risk data, high-dependency systems, hard-to-change platforms, trust anchors, and vendor-controlled components.

15
00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:45.329
It should also identify lower-risk areas where routine lifecycle updates may be enough.

16
00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:49.564
The point is to avoid both complacency and uncontrolled urgency.

17
00:01:49.564 --> 00:01:55.107
Quantum readiness should be disciplined, evidence-based, and tied to real systems.
